  "strategy": "Let me propose a new approach for the step function minimization using a mathematical optimization technique called Frank-Wolfe with momentum and adaptive feature selection. This approach will systematically identify and target specific features that contribute to the high convolution peaks.\n\n## Approach and rationale\nThe key insight is that the convex combination step in the standard mirror descent can be generalized by explicitly selecting and exploiting specific features from the first-order condition. We can interpret the dual variable as a \"surrogate gradient\" and directly select its sparsest, highest-amplitude components to drive the optimization.\n\nThis approach reduces the parameter space complexity while increasing the neighborhood search efficiency. By focusing on discrete amplitude changes rather than continuous smoothing, we can accelerate convergence and find better solutions in less time.\n\n## Mathematical specification\nWe maintain a population of sequences with fixed dimensionality (initially 256) but allow higher-dimensional projections through feature selection. The main components are:\n\n1. Initialization function: Creates a base sequence with maximum value at index 0 and decaying values.\n2. Feature bottleneck analysis: Identifies the most significant indices for amplitude reduction.\n3.\"Gradient\" calculation: Selects extreme components from the dual variable to drive optimization.\n4.Momentum-assisted update: Includes a directional bias component to avoid local minima.\n5.Promotion factor: Dynamically adjusts the effect size to target high-impact features.\n\nThe objective function is: \n    f(x) = 2*N*max(b) / (sum(a))^2\nwhere:\n    - N = length of the sequence\n    - b = |convolve(a, a)| (mode=\"full\" indices)\n\n## Implementation procedure\n1.Call evaluate_sequence to verify the initial sequence is valid.\n2.Perform \"gradient\" analysis on the dual variables to identify high-impact components.\n3.Design a promotion/selection mechanism that prioritizes indices based on their effect.\n4.Implement momentum in the update steps.\n5.Handling edge cases:\n   - No candidate found: Fall back to initialized sequence.\n   - Convergence: Switch to more aggressive feature selection when stuck.\n\n### Detailed Steps\n\n2. Prepare initial sequence (if provided)\n   - Use parent sequence of length N (if available)\n   - Otherwise use default initialization (Princeton Bell curve)\n\n3. Within the time limit (1800 seconds):\n   a.Promotion phase:\n      - Already implemented in previous strategy\n   b.Direct feature selection:\n      - I\u0628\u0637icipate(feature_detection):\n        ```\n        def feature_detection(a):\n            N = len(a)\n            conv = fast_convolve(a)\n            peak_val = np.max(conv)\n            # Identify the most significant contributing features\n            # Calculate the impact potential for each index\n            # Use these amplitudes to select momentum components\n        ```\n   c.Mirror descent with feature projection:\n        - The current vector is updated in direction of selected features\n        - Use adaptive step size based on the brand-new projected score\n\n4.Termination conditions:\n   - 600 iterations with no improvement\n   - Time limit reached\n   - Initial sequence found better than candidates\n\n### Search choices and useful variations\nEach iteration starts by:\n- Evaluating the current sequence with evaluate_sequence(*args)\n- Calculating the dual gradient (peak to average ratio)\n\nWe then select up to 6 of the most impactful indices to form the update direction.\n\nDuring the entire 1800 seconds, we allocate time for:\n\n1.Selection base optimization (60%)\n2.Adaptive feature selection (20%)\n3.Momentum injection (10%)\n4.Circular convolution avoidance filter (5%)\n\n### Compute budget and robustness\nWe anticipate:\n\n```\ntime_0 = time.monotonic()\nbase_points = 256\ngenerate_sequence = initial_seq_points\nwhile time_left > 0 and not terminated:\n\n    # 1. Update using direct selection\n    current_eval = evaluate_sequence(seq.tolist())\n    if current_eval < best_score:\n        best_score = current_eval\n        best_seq = seq\n\n    # 2. Identify new candidate indices\n    grad_f = sum of gradients (promoted amplitudes)\n    grad_d = sum of directional components (6 most significant)\n\n    # 3. Compute step size based on internal state\n    alpha = min(0.2 * n, 0.9)   # To ensure new indices are introduced\n\n    # 4. Check for termination:\n    if not\u6539\u5584\uff1a\n        print(\"Stuck: Increasing dimension or try different initialization\")\n        # Fallback to full-dimensional sequence\n\n    # 5. Update using gradient components and new projected features\n    seq = alpha * grad_d + (1-alpha) * original_sequence\n\n    # 6. Normalize by total sum\n    total_sum = sum(seq)\n    if total_sum < 1e-10: normalize to 1.0\n    seq = seq / total_sum\n\n    # 7. Safety check: Ensure all values non-negative and below 1000\n    new_seq = np.maximum(seq, 0)[:, 0:min(max_points, len(seq))]\n```\n\n### Validation and return contract\n\nWe ensure the final result:\n\n- Is a list of 256 coefficients (optimized and valid)\n- Passes evaluate_sequence internal checks\n\nIf the convex hull of features requires extending the dimension:\n\n```\nif dimension < 256:\n    expand_to = 256\n    kernel = \u79fb\u690d of_promotion(x, expansion_factor=1.5, dimension=expand_to)\n    x = np.concatenate([x, kernel])\n```\n\nBut note we are constrained to a fixed-dimensional space in analysis.\n\n### Length variability and fallback\nIf target length changes:\n\n```\ndef length_adjust(base, target_len):\n    # Use minimally invasive linear interpolation for dimensional change\n    if target_len <= base_len:\n        # Collapse sequence to target_len\n        # Reduce the sum of squares while maintaining non-negativity\n        dscore = evaluate_sequence(sequence[:target_len])\n        if dscore < best_score:\n            best_seq = sequence[:target_len]\n            best_score = dscore\n    else:\n        # Expand uniformly with zero-padding (contradiction for objective)\n        # But the objective is defined for varying lengths, so we must expand\n        # We'll use interpolation (minimum ormax?) coefficients\n        pass\n```\n\n### Fallback\nIf no candidate sequence is found within deadline, we return the initial_princetonseq.\n\nThis approach combines multiple benefits:\n\n1.Increased information flow\n2.Momentum in dual directions\n3.Focused feature search\n4.Dimension optimization\n5.Robust fallback",
